Source paper: https://www.whatdotheyknow.com/request/oxford_physics_past_papers_2/response/2340696/attach/4/Physics%20Papers%202015.pdf?cookie_passthrough=1. The held cover dates this sitting to 19 April 2016; the annual bundle's filename does not determine the year. We use $d=4-\epsilon$ for the ultraviolet regulator, reserving $i0$ for the causal prescription. The supplied propagators correspond to $g=\operatorname{diag}(-,+,+,+)$ and $\{\gamma^\mu,\gamma^\nu\}=-2g^{\mu\nu}$. Q1 and Q3 are readable. A duplicate cover obscures Q2 on printed page 3; its known a–c scopes retain explicit source-gap TODOs.

== 1
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

Choose the <covariant derivative> $D_\mu=\partial_\mu+i\mu^{\epsilon/2}eA_\mu$, so the <interaction term> is $-\mu^{\epsilon/2}e\bar\psi\gamma^\mu A_\mu\psi$. The <gauge transformation> is $A_\mu\mapsto A_\mu+\partial_\mu\alpha$ and $\psi\mapsto e^{-i\mu^{\epsilon/2}e\alpha}\psi$. The derivative of this local phase cancels the added vector-potential term in $D_\mu\psi$, making $i\bar\psi\gamma^\mu D_\mu\psi$ <gauge invariant>.

For renormalized fields and parameters, a complete local <QED> <counterterm> parametrization is
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\mathcal L={}&i\bar\psi\not\partial\psi-m\bar\psi\psi-\frac14F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}
-\mu^{\epsilon/2}e\bar\psi\gamma^\mu A_\mu\psi+\mathcal L_{\rm ct},\\
\mathcal L_{\rm ct}={}&i\delta Z_2\bar\psi\not\partial\psi-\delta m_L\bar\psi\psi
-\frac{\delta Z_3}{4}F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}
-\mu^{\epsilon/2}e\delta Z_1\bar\psi\gamma^\mu A_\mu\psi.
\end{aligned}}
$$
With $\psi_0=\sqrt{Z_2}\psi$, $A_0=\sqrt{Z_3}A$, the definitions are $\delta Z_i=Z_i-1$, $\delta m_L=Z_2m_0-m$, and $e_0=\mu^{\epsilon/2}eZ_1/(Z_2\sqrt{Z_3})$. In particular $\delta m_L$ is the coefficient in the <Lagrangian density>, not just $m_0-m$.

The <Ward identity> requires $Z_1=Z_2$ in a gauge-preserving <renormalization scheme>. The kinetic and vertex <counterterms> then combine into $i\delta Z_2\bar\psi\not D\psi$, so <gauge invariance> is explicit and $e_0=\mu^{\epsilon/2}e/\sqrt{Z_3}$. A <photon> <mass> term is not an allowed <counterterm> in unbroken <QED>. For perturbative inversion one separately adds <gauge fixing>, $\mathcal L_{\rm gf}=-(\partial_\mu A^\mu)^2/(2\xi)$, taking $\xi=1$ in <Feynman gauge>. That quantization term is not itself part of the gauge-invariant density. The Abelian <Faddeev-Popov determinant> is field independent for this linear gauge and introduces no interacting ghost contribution.

==== ii
{scope}

===== Solution

At second order in the <interaction term>, attach the two external <fermion> fields to different vertices and contract the remaining two <fermion> fields with each other and the two vector fields with each other. The factor $1/2!$ in the expansion is cancelled by the two ways to assign the external ends. There is no closed <fermion loop>, and hence no extra closed-loop <fermionic sign>.

After removing the two external <Dirac propagators>, the internal <momentum> assignment $l$ for the <photon> and $p+l$ for the <fermion> gives the one-loop insertion
$$
\boxed{\mathcal I(p)=(-ie\mu^{\epsilon/2})^2\int\frac{d^dl}{(2\pi)^d}
\gamma^\nu\widetilde S(p+l)\gamma^\mu\widetilde\Delta_{\mu\nu}(l)
=-e^2\mu^\epsilon\int\frac{d^dl}{(2\pi)^d}
\gamma^\nu\widetilde S(p+l)\gamma^\mu\widetilde\Delta_{\mu\nu}(l).}
$$
Thus the insertion is proportional to exactly the propagator product specified in the source. Define $\mathcal I=-i\Sigma$. With Fourier convention $\psi(x)\sim e^{ip\cdot x}$, the reduced free inverse <kernel> is $K_0(p)=-\not p-m$, since the printed <Dirac propagator> equals $iK_0^{-1}$. Repeated insertions give
$$
S=i(K_0-\Sigma)^{-1},\qquad
\Gamma^{(2)}=K_0-\Sigma+K_{\rm ct}.
$$
The factors of $i$ and the sign of the named <self-energy> are convention dependent; the cancelling <counterterm> is fixed once this insertion convention is specified. The internal <photon> joins two vertices on one directed <fermion> line, as in the first panel of the figure in 1b.

==== iii
{scope}

===== Solution

Multiply the supplied <Feynman gauge> propagators before reducing the numerator. Their two factors $-i$ and the source's <Clifford algebra> imply
$$
\gamma^\nu\widetilde S(p+l)\gamma^\mu\widetilde\Delta_{\mu\nu}(l)
=\frac{(d-2)(\not p+\not l)+dm}
{(l^2-i0)[(p+l)^2+m^2-i0]}.
$$
For example, contracting the numerator before the propagator factors gives $\gamma^\nu[-(\not p+\not l)+m]\gamma_\nu=-(d-2)(\not p+\not l)-dm$; the extra minus from $(-i)^2$ reverses both signs.

Use a <Feynman parameter> $x$ on the massive denominator and shift $q=l+xp$. The combined denominator is
$$
x[(p+l)^2+m^2]+(1-x)l^2=q^2+D(x),\qquad
D(x)=xm^2+x(1-x)p^2.
$$
The term odd in $q$ integrates to zero in <dimensional regularization>. The pole can be evaluated away from on-shell infrared singularities; its local ultraviolet coefficient is independent of $D$. With the source's master integral,
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal I_{\rm UV}(p)
&=-\frac{ie^2}{8\pi^2\epsilon}\int_0^1dx\,[2(1-x)\not p+4m]\\
&=-\frac{ie^2}{8\pi^2\epsilon}(\not p+4m),\qquad
\Sigma_{\rm UV}(p)=\frac{e^2}{8\pi^2\epsilon}(\not p+4m).
\end{aligned}
$$
Terms proportional to $d-4$ multiply the pole into a finite term, so they do not change this result. The <counterterm> <kernel> from 1ai is $K_{\rm ct}=-\delta Z_2\not p-\delta m_L$. Cancelling $-\Sigma_{\rm UV}+K_{\rm ct}$ therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\delta Z_2=-\frac{e^2}{8\pi^2\epsilon},\qquad
\delta m_L=-\frac{e^2m}{2\pi^2\epsilon}.}
$$
The distinction between <mass> definitions matters. Since $Z_2m_0=m+\delta m_L$,
$$
\boxed{m_0-m=\delta m_L-m\delta Z_2=-\frac{3e^2m}{8\pi^2\epsilon},\qquad
Z_m-1=-\frac{3e^2}{8\pi^2\epsilon}\quad(m_0=Z_mm).}
$$
Equivalently the mass-dependent part of the <counterterm> density is $+e^2m\bar\psi\psi/(2\pi^2\epsilon)$, because its definition includes the explicit minus sign in $-\delta m_L\bar\psi\psi$. In the alternative convention $d=4-2\varepsilon$, replace every $1/\epsilon$ here by $1/(2\varepsilon)$. These are ultraviolet minimal-subtraction poles, not the finite on-shell <mass> correction. The <Ward identity> also fixes $\delta Z_1=\delta Z_2$; the <photon> coefficient $\delta Z_3$ requires its own two-point function and is not obtained from this graph.

=== b
{scope}

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

In pure <QCD>, different incoming and outgoing <quark flavours> allow only the tree-level $s$-channel <gluon> graph. A <quark-gluon vertex> preserves flavour, so no flavour-changing $t$-channel exchange is available. Label the incoming momenta $p_1,p_2$ and outgoing momenta $k_1,k_2$. For transparent <spin> <traces>, use the auxiliary metric $\eta=-g$ and its usual <gamma matrices>; the positive-energy <Mandelstam variables> are
$$
s=(p_1+p_2)_\eta^2,\qquad t=(p_1-k_1)_\eta^2,\qquad u=(p_1-k_2)_\eta^2,\qquad s+t+u=0.
$$
This is a consistent change of metric notation, not a change of the physical invariants. Define the fixed-index colour tensor $C_{ji;kl}=\sum_a(T^a)_{ji}(T^a)_{kl}$. Up to an irrelevant common phase,
$$
\mathcal M=\frac{g_s^2}{s}C_{ji;kl}
[\bar v(p_2)\gamma^\mu u(p_1)]
[\bar u(k_1)\gamma_\mu v(k_2)].
$$
The indices $i,j$ label incoming colours and $k,l$ outgoing colours. Averaging over the two <spins> of each incoming <particle> and summing the outgoing <spins> gives
$$
\overline{|\mathcal M|^2}_{\rm spin}
=\frac{g_s^4|C_{ji;kl}|^2}{4s^2}
\operatorname{tr}(\not p_2\gamma^\mu\not p_1\gamma^\nu)
\operatorname{tr}(\not k_1\gamma_\mu\not k_2\gamma_\nu).
$$
The <gamma-matrix trace> identity makes the contraction
$$
32[(p_1\cdot k_1)(p_2\cdot k_2)+(p_1\cdot k_2)(p_2\cdot k_1)]
=8(t^2+u^2).
$$
For instance $p_1\cdot k_1=p_2\cdot k_2=-t/2$ and the crossed products are $-u/2$. Therefore, before the remaining colour sum and average,
$$
\boxed{\overline{|\mathcal M|^2}_{\rm spin}
=2g_s^4|C_{ji;kl}|^2\frac{t^2+u^2}{s^2}.}
$$
Here the bar averages <spins> only; it does not silently include the colour average requested next.

\Image[2016-qed-self-energy-and-quark-annihilation.png]
{title=One-loop QED fermion self-energy and different-flavour quark annihilation}
{description=The left panel shows the internal fermion and photon in the self-energy insertion. The right panel is the single pure-QCD tree topology for the specified flavour-changing pair final state; arrows show fermion-number flow.}

==== ii
{scope}

===== Solution

Use the fundamental <SU(3)> <normalization> $\operatorname{tr}(T^aT^b)=\delta^{ab}/2$, with <Hermitian> generators. The average over the $3\times3$ incoming colours and sum over the outgoing colours gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
C_{\rm col}
&=\frac1{N_c^2}\sum_{i,j,k,l}\left|\sum_a(T^a)_{ji}(T^a)_{kl}\right|^2\\
&=\frac1{N_c^2}\sum_{a,b}\operatorname{tr}(T^aT^b)\operatorname{tr}(T^aT^b)
=\frac{N_c^2-1}{4N_c^2}=\frac29\qquad(N_c=3).
\end{aligned}
$$
The complete <spin> and colour averaged result is consequently
$$
\boxed{\overline{|\mathcal M|^2}
=\frac{4g_s^4}{9}\frac{t^2+u^2}{s^2}
=\frac{64\pi^2\alpha_s^2}{9}\frac{t^2+u^2}{s^2},\qquad
\alpha_s=\frac{g_s^2}{4\pi}.}
$$
For massless two-body centre-of-momentum <kinematics>, $t=-s(1-\cos\theta)/2$ and $u=-s(1+\cos\theta)/2$, so the same answer is $2g_s^4(1+\cos^2\theta)/9$. This checks positivity, the symmetry under exchanging the outgoing directions, and the absence of a forward $t$-channel singularity in this particular process.

