== 3
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

Work entirely with left-handed <Weyl fermions>; a right-handed field is counted through its left-handed charge conjugate. For a simple non-Abelian group $G$, define
$$
\operatorname{tr}_{R}(T^aT^b)=T(R)\delta^{ab},\qquad
D_R^{abc}=\operatorname{tr}_{R}[T^a\{T^b,T^c\}].
$$
The two orientations of a chiral triangle supply the symmetrized <trace>. Spectator representation dimensions multiply each <trace>. The perturbative <gauge anomaly> conditions for $G\times U(1)$ are therefore
$$
\boxed{\sum_iD_{R_i}^{abc}=0\quad\text{for every }a,b,c,\qquad
\sum_iQ_iT(R_i)=0,\qquad
\sum_i\dim(R_i)Q_i^3=0.}
$$
The first concerns $G^3$, the second $G^2U(1)$, and the third $U(1)^3$. A $GU(1)^2$ triangle has group factor $\sum_iQ_i^2\operatorname{tr}_{R_i}T^a=0$, because non-Abelian generators of a semisimple group are traceless. If coupling to gravity is required, also impose the mixed gauge-gravitational condition $\sum_i\dim(R_i)Q_i=0$. Cancellation of these local triangles is not a universal substitute for checking global anomalies; the <Witten SU(2) anomaly> is the relevant additional test here.<Gauge Theory by David Tong>[§§3.4.1–3.4.4: local gauge anomalies, mixed gravitational condition, <SU(2)> <global anomaly> and <Standard Model>]{ref}

For the <Standard Model>, one generation consists of $Q_L:(3,2)_{1/6}$, $u^c:(\bar3,1)_{-2/3}$, $d^c:(\bar3,1)_{1/3}$, $L_L:(1,2)_{-1/2}$ and $e^c:(1,1)_1$. These are precisely the all-left-handed representations; conjugating the singlets reverses their <hypercharges> relative to right-handed <particles>. Check the requested triangles separately:

For $3$–$3$–$3$, normalize the cubic coefficient of a fundamental to $+1$. Its conjugate has $-1$, so the weak-doublet multiplicity gives
$$
\mathcal A_{333}=2-1-1=0.
$$
For $2$–$2$–$2$, the doublet generators obey $\{T^b,T^c\}=\delta^{bc}I/2$, so $\operatorname{tr}(T^a\{T^b,T^c\})=0$. Thus every doublet separately has zero local cubic weak anomaly. For the global <Witten SU(2) anomaly>, each generation has three coloured <quark> doublets and one lepton doublet, an even total of four. All three generations give twelve, so the global obstruction also cancels.

For $3$–$3$–$1$, use $T(3)=T(\bar3)=1/2$. The two conjugate triplets have the same quadratic index, not its negative:
$$
\mathcal A_{331}=2\left(\frac16\right)\frac12
+\left(-\frac23\right)\frac12
+\left(\frac13\right)\frac12=0.
$$
For $2$–$2$–$1$, the three <quark> colours and one lepton doublet give
$$
\mathcal A_{221}=3\left(\frac16\right)\frac12
+\left(-\frac12\right)\frac12=0.
$$
For $1$–$1$–$1$, count every coloured and weak component:
$$
\mathcal A_{111}=6\left(\frac16\right)^3+3\left(-\frac23\right)^3
+3\left(\frac13\right)^3+2\left(-\frac12\right)^3+1
=\frac{1-32+4-9+36}{36}=0.
$$
The mixed gauge-gravitational coefficient also vanishes:
$$
6\left(\frac16\right)+3\left(-\frac23\right)+3\left(\frac13\right)
+2\left(-\frac12\right)+1=0.
$$
Unlisted gauge combinations contain a single generator of at least one simple factor. For example, $3$–$2$–$1$ has $\operatorname{tr}_3T^a\operatorname{tr}_2t^b\,Y$, and $3$–$3$–$2$ has $\operatorname{tr}_3(T^aT^b)\operatorname{tr}_2t^c$; both vanish. The same reasoning covers $3$–$1$–$1$, $2$–$1$–$1$ and $3$–$2$–$2$, including permutations of the legs. <Scalars> contribute no chiral triangle anomaly. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\text{All the listed gauge anomalies cancel within each generation; three generations remain anomaly free.}}
$$

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

For $\lambda>0$, the <scalar potential> is minimized at $\phi^\dagger\phi=v^2/2$. With the specified upper-component <vacuum expectation value> $\phi_0=(v,0)^T/\sqrt2$ and <hypercharge> $Y=-I/2$, the unbroken generator is
$$
Q=T^3+Y=\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix},\qquad Q\phi_0=0.
$$
The other three independent generator directions act nontrivially on $\phi_0$. Thus the connected unbroken gauge subgroup is $U(1)_{\rm em}$, while three gauge directions are broken. The <normalization> here is $Q=T^3+Y$, not the alternative convention in which <hypercharge> is doubled.

Apply the <covariant derivative> directly to the vacuum:
$$
D_\mu\phi_0=-\frac{iv}{2\sqrt2}
\begin{pmatrix}g_2A_\mu^3-g_1B_\mu\\g_2(A_\mu^1+iA_\mu^2)\end{pmatrix}.
$$
In the mostly-plus convention the canonical <scalar> kinetic term is $-(D_\mu\phi)^\dagger D^\mu\phi$. Its quadratic vector terms are
$$
\mathcal L_{\rm mass}=-\frac{v^2}{8}
\left[g_2^2(A_\mu^1A^{1\mu}+A_\mu^2A^{2\mu})
+(g_2A_\mu^3-g_1B_\mu)(g_2A^{3\mu}-g_1B^\mu)\right].
$$
Define $s_W=\sin\theta_W=g_1/\sqrt{g_1^2+g_2^2}$ and $c_W=\cos\theta_W=g_2/\sqrt{g_1^2+g_2^2}$. Diagonalization gives
$$
\boxed{W_\mu^\pm=\frac{A_\mu^1\mp iA_\mu^2}{\sqrt2},\qquad
Z_\mu=c_WA_\mu^3-s_WB_\mu,\qquad
A_\mu^\gamma=s_WA_\mu^3+c_WB_\mu.}
$$
The neutral combination $Z$ lies along the nonzero <eigenvector> $(g_2,-g_1)$; the orthogonal combination $(g_1,g_2)$ is the massless <photon>. Comparing the <mass> terms with $-m_W^2W^+\cdot W^--m_Z^2Z^2/2$ yields
$$
\boxed{m_W=\frac{g_2v}{2},\qquad
m_Z=\frac v2\sqrt{g_1^2+g_2^2},\qquad
m_\gamma=0,\qquad\frac{m_W}{m_Z}=\cos\theta_W.}
$$
Finally insert the inverse neutral rotation into the coupling:
$$
g_2T^3A_\mu^3+g_1YB_\mu
=(g_2s_WT^3+g_1c_WY)A_\mu^\gamma
+(g_2c_WT^3-g_1s_WY)Z_\mu.
$$
Both coefficients in the <photon> term agree, giving
$$
\boxed{e=g_2\sin\theta_W=g_1\cos\theta_W
=\frac{g_1g_2}{\sqrt{g_1^2+g_2^2}},\qquad D_\mu\supset-ieQA_\mu^\gamma.}
$$
The upper vacuum component is electrically neutral and the lower <scalar> component has charge $-e$, consistently with the source's negative <hypercharge>. This doublet is the conjugate convention to the commonly displayed positive-hypercharge doublet; it does not change the vector masses or the electromagnetic coupling. The four real <scalar> components supply three would-be <Goldstone modes> and one physical radial Higgs mode, preserving the total physical degrees of freedom.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

For $M>0$, integrate the vector action by parts, dropping the boundary term, and write it as a quadratic functional:
$$
S_0[A]=\frac12\int d^4x\,A_\mu K^{\mu\nu}A_\nu,\qquad
K^{\mu\nu}=(\Box-M^2)g^{\mu\nu}-\partial^\mu\partial^\nu.
$$
The explicit <mass> makes this Proca <kernel> invertible: the displayed action alone has no gauge redundancy left to fix. It is interpreted as <unitary gauge> when obtained from a Higgs or Stückelberg completion with its would-be Goldstone field removed.

Introduce a source $J_\mu$ and complete the square, $A=A'-K^{-1}J$. The normalized Gaussian <path integral> is
$$
\boxed{\frac{Z[J]}{Z[0]}=\exp\left[-\frac i2\int d^4x\,d^4y\,
J_\mu(x)(K^{-1})^{\mu\nu}(x,y)J_\nu(y)\right],\qquad
\widetilde D_{\mu\nu}=i(\widetilde K^{-1})_{\mu\nu}.}
$$
The source-independent <functional determinant> cancels in $Z[J]/Z[0]$. For Fourier <momentum> $k$, the mixed-index <kernel> is $\widetilde K^\mu{}_{\nu}=-(k^2+M^2)\delta^\mu{}_{\nu}+k^\mu k_\nu$. For $k^2\ne0$, use the <projectors>
$$
P_L{}^\mu{}_{\nu}=\frac{k^\mu k_\nu}{k^2},\qquad P_T=I-P_L.
$$
Their orthogonality gives
$$
\widetilde K=-(k^2+M^2)P_T-M^2P_L,\qquad
\widetilde K^{-1}=-\frac{P_T}{k^2+M^2}-\frac{P_L}{M^2}.
$$
Combining the <projectors> removes their apparent massless denominator. Adding the Feynman prescription gives the unitary-gauge propagator
$$
\boxed{\widetilde D_{\mu\nu}(k)=\frac{-i}{k^2+M^2-i0}
\left(g_{\mu\nu}+\frac{k_\mu k_\nu}{M^2}\right).}
$$
Multiplication by $\widetilde K$ verifies that it inverts the quadratic operator. On the physical pole $k^2=-M^2$, the numerator is transverse to $k$, as required for the three physical massive-vector polarizations.

The drawback is its off-shell ultraviolet behaviour: at large spacelike <momentum> the transverse term falls as $1/k^2$, but the longitudinal tensor term remains of order $1/M^2$. Naive loop <power counting> therefore looks worse and <renormalizability> is not manifest. In a finite $R_\xi$ gauge the corresponding expression is
$$
D_\xi=-i\left[\frac{P_T}{k^2+M^2-i0}+\frac{\xi P_L}{k^2+\xi M^2-i0}\right],
$$
which falls as $1/k^2$ at fixed finite $\xi$. Its $\xi\to\infty$ limit gives the unitary-gauge longitudinal term, so taking that limit before the ultraviolet limit changes <power counting>.

This is not a claim that a renormalizable Higgs theory ceases to be physically renormalizable in this gauge. Nor does it mean that the free Proca <Gaussian integral> is inconsistent. For conserved external sources $k\cdot J=0$, the longitudinal numerator drops out of $J^\mu D_{\mu\nu}J^\nu$ and the tree exchange again falls as $1/k^2$. The difficulty concerns off-shell <Green functions> and manifest control of loop divergences, not the number of physical polarizations.
